[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    13[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    14[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Findings that shape the table:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    15[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    16[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Every public function returns `void`. There is **no** error code, no sentinel
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    17[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   return, no `errno` write, no `assert`, no error enum, no `RETURN_ERROR` macro,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    18[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   and no `exit`/`abort` path anywhere in the library.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    19[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * Therefore the library's entire *rejection* surface is the single guarded
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    20[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   branch at `driver.c:32` — `printLine` silently does nothing when handed a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    21[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   null pointer. "Same error/rejection" for this API means **same observable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    22[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   effect**, i.e. identical bytes on `stdout` (and no crash).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    23[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * The remaining rows below are the generic FFI boundaries the task requires:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    24[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   null pointers, zero/oversized lengths, and values one past a valid range.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    25[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `driver`'s `int useGood` is the only enum-like parameter crossing the FFI
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    26[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   boundary; C accepts any `int` there, so out-of-range "enum" values
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    27[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   (`INT_MIN`, `-1`, `2`, `INT_MAX`, …) are real inputs and each must select the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    28[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   same branch in Rust as in C (`!= 0` → `good`, `== 0` → `bad`).
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    29[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    30[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | # | function | trigger (the exact invalid input/condition) | expected C result | test | [x] |
